  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f(x),$ defined below?\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3},\\,\\, & x<2 \\\\ 8,\\,\\, & x=2 \\\\ 2{x}^{2},\\, & x>2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $f(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=2$\n- $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=2$\n- $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2$",
  "worked_solution": "We're required to determine the left and right continuity properties of $f(x)$ at $x=2.$\n\nFirst, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2) & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The function is left-continuous at $x=2$ since the left-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & ={2}^{3} \\\\  & =8 \\\\  & =f(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function is right-continuous at $x=2$ since the right-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & =2(2)^{2} \\\\  & =8 \\\\  & =f(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. The function is both left and right-continuous at $x=2,$ which implies that the function is continuous at $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"",
